To replace all instances of "erpnext.healthcare." with "pets_healthcare." in your code, you can use the sed command in a Unix-based terminal. Here's how you can do it for all files within your project directory:

Open a terminal.
Navigate to the root directory of your project.
Run the following sed command:
sh
Copy code
find . -type f -exec sed -i 's/erpnext\.healthcare\./pets_healthcare\./g' {} +
This command does the following:

find . -type f: Finds all files in the current directory and its subdirectories.
-exec sed -i 's/erpnext\.healthcare\./pets_healthcare\./g' {} +: Executes the sed command on each file found, replacing all occurrences of erpnext.healthcare. with pets_healthcare..
Note: Be careful when running this command, as it will modify all files in the directory. Make sure to have a backup of your files or use version control to track changes.

