To perform both replacements in all files within your project directory, you can use the following sed commands in a Unix-based terminal. Make sure you have a backup of your files before running these commands.

Replacing erpnext.healthcare. with pets_healthcare.pets_healthcare.
Open a terminal.
Navigate to the root directory of your project.
Run the following command:
sh
Copy code
find . -type f -exec sed -i 's/erpnext\.healthcare\./pets_healthcare\.pets_healthcare\./g' {} +
Replacing erpnext.setup.doctype.employee with erpnext.hr.doctype.employee
In the same terminal session, run the following command:
sh
Copy code
find . -type f -exec sed -i 's/erpnext\.setup\.doctype\.employee/erpnext\.hr\.doctype\.employee/g' {} +
These commands do the following:

find . -type f: Finds all files in the current directory and its subdirectories.
-exec sed -i 's/old_text/new_text/g' {} +: Executes the sed command on each file found, replacing all occurrences of old_text with new_text.
Both commands will apply the respective replacements across all files in the directory tree. If you have specific file types or directories to target, you can modify the find command accordingly.

